WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:20.750
My heart pounded with terror! The horrible squid had Ned pinned to the deck and was about to attack him with its terrible beak.

2
00:00:20.751 --> 00:00:27.797
I rushed to Ned's aid, but Captain Nemo got there before me and slashed at the squid's tentacles with his hatchet.

3
00:00:27.798 --> 00:00:31.718
Ned broke free and killed the squid with his harpoon.

4
00:00:31.719 --> 00:00:40.551
The battle with the squid lasted fifteen minutes before the monsters, vanquished and mutilated, slid off the Nautilus and slipped under the waves.

5
00:00:40.552 --> 00:00:47.043
Captain Nemo gazed at the sea that had swallowed up one of his crewmen, and his eyes filled with tears.

6
00:00:47.044 --> 00:00:56.114
This was the second companion he had lost since our arrival onboard his submarine, but this unfortunate man would not be buried in the coral cemetery.

7
00:00:56.115 --> 00:01:01.686
Captain Nemo retired to his cabin, and I didn't see him for quite a few days.

8
00:01:01.687 --> 00:01:06.027
However I could tell that he remained sad and indecisive.

9
00:01:06.028 --> 00:01:11.265
He was the heart and soul of the Nautilus, and the vessel mirrored his emotions.

10
00:01:11.266 --> 00:01:16.344
When he was shut up in his cabin, the submarine floated at random upon the waves.

11
00:01:16.345 --> 00:01:24.424
It seemed as if Captain Nemo could not tear himself away from the scene of this last battle, from this sea that had devoured one of his men.

12
00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:31.336
We passed ten days like this until May 1, when the Nautilus resumed its northerly course.

13
00:01:31.337 --> 00:01:37.431
Now we were following the Gulf Stream, which flows like a river through the Atlantic Ocean.

14
00:01:37.432 --> 00:01:42.415
A week later we were off the coast of North Carolina in the United States.

15
00:01:42.416 --> 00:01:47.408
Once more the vessel seemed to be moving at random with no one in charge.

16
00:01:47.409 --> 00:01:52.574
Under these circumstances, I thought that escape might be possible.

17
00:01:52.575 --> 00:01:57.050
Perhaps we could be picked up by one of the many ships sailing these waters.

18
00:01:57.051 --> 00:02:00.804
There was only one problem: the weather was horrible.

19
00:02:00.805 --> 00:02:08.820
We were in waters where hurricanes are often found, and to attempt an escape in a frail boat was to risk certain destruction.

20
00:02:08.821 --> 00:02:13.884
Even Ned admitted this, although he didn't give up the idea of escape altogether.

21
00:02:13.885 --> 00:02:20.789
"Professor, this voyage must come to an end," he said to me as we descended from the platform.

22
00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:27.559
"I followed Captain Nemo to the South Pole, but I declare I won't follow him to the North Pole."

23
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.297
"What do you suggest we do since we can't escape right now?"

24
00:02:31.298 --> 00:02:41.876
"We must speak to the captain. If we continue northward, we will soon be near Canada, my homeland. It makes me furious just to think about it!"

25
00:02:41.877 --> 00:02:46.789
Ned's moods had grown gloomier, and his face had changed in appearance.

26
00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:51.257
I understood Ned's suffering because I was feeling homesick myself.

27
00:02:51.258 --> 00:02:55.845
Six months had passed without our having any news from land.

28
00:02:55.846 --> 00:03:04.004
The change in Captain Nemo's personality since the battle with the squid had further diminished my enthusiasm for life aboard the submarine.

29
00:03:04.005 --> 00:03:08.966
"Well?" said Ned when I didn't reply. "Are you going to speak to the captain or not?

30
00:03:08.967 --> 00:03:12.845
You can tell him that I want to know if he's ever going to set us free."

31
00:03:12.846 --> 00:03:22.242
"He has already told us that will never happen," I reminded Ned, "and besides, I never see the captain anymore. He seems to avoid me."

32
00:03:22.243 --> 00:03:26.670
"Then you must go to see him at once," said Ned.

33
00:03:26.671 --> 00:03:33.662
I returned to my cabin, which adjoined Captain Nemo's, and decided to take action before I lost my nerve.

34
00:03:33.663 --> 00:03:39.821
I knocked at his door, but there was no answer. I knocked again, and then turned the handle.

35
00:03:39.822 --> 00:03:45.233
The door opened, and I entered to find the captain bending over his work at the table.

36
00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:50.218
He raised his head quickly, frowned, and said harshly, "What are you doing here?"

37
00:03:50.219 --> 00:03:52.559
"I need to speak to you, Captain."

38
00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:58.114
"But I'm busy working. I don't disturb you when you're working. Shouldn't you show me the same courtesy?"

39
00:03:58.115 --> 00:04:01.892
This reception was not encouraging, but I pressed on.

40
00:04:01.893 --> 00:04:05.463
"Captain, I need to speak to you about an urgent matter."

41
00:04:05.464 --> 00:04:13.884
"What?" he said sarcastically. "Have you made some new discovery? Has the sea shown you something that man has never seen before?"

42
00:04:13.885 --> 00:04:21.535
The conversation wasn't going well at all, but before I could reply, he showed me an open manuscript on his table.

43
00:04:21.536 --> 00:04:28.281
"Here, Professor Aronnax, is a manuscript that summarizes all my studies of the sea.

44
00:04:28.282 --> 00:04:32.884
It's written in several languages, and it will not perish with me.

45
00:04:32.885 --> 00:04:41.091
This manuscript, accompanied by my real name and my life story, will be enclosed in a small unsinkable capsule.

46
00:04:41.092 --> 00:04:49.750
The last survivor of those men onboard the Nautilus will throw the capsule into the sea, and the water will carry it to some distant shore."

47
00:04:49.751 --> 00:04:56.988
So he was going to reveal his name and his mysterious past! That would be a book worth reading.

48
00:04:56.989 --> 00:05:02.821
"Captain," I tried again. "I appreciate that you want the world to read your manuscript.

49
00:05:02.822 --> 00:05:11.662
The results of your studies must not be lost, but who knows where the wind and waves will take your book? Surely you or one of your—"

50
00:05:11.663 --> 00:05:15.313
"Never, Professor!" he interrupted me.

51
00:05:15.314 --> 00:05:21.631
"Perhaps my companions and I could keep it for you, if you set us free and we—"

52
00:05:21.632 --> 00:05:25.154
"Set you free?" The captain stood up and faced me.

53
00:05:25.155 --> 00:05:28.813
"Yes, sir, that's what I want to talk to you about.

54
00:05:28.814 --> 00:05:34.892
We've been onboard the Nautilus for six months, and we need to know if you intend to keep us here forever."

55
00:05:34.893 --> 00:05:38.559
"Professor, I'll tell you the same thing I told you six months ago.

56
00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.813
Whoever boards this submarine must never leave it." Captain Nemo crossed his arms.

57
00:05:43.814 --> 00:05:51.130
"I don't speak only for myself, but also for my companions. Ned is nearly at the end of his rope.

58
00:05:51.131 --> 00:05:55.972
I don't know what he'll do if you keep him here much longer, but I know he's desperate."

59
00:05:55.973 --> 00:06:00.035
"What does it matter to me what Ned thinks or does?" said the captain.

60
00:06:00.036 --> 00:06:05.035
"Now, Professor, let this be the last time we discuss this matter."

61
00:06:05.036 --> 00:06:12.766
I turned and left the captain's quarters. I knew our situation was hopeless and I had to tell my companions what I'd just heard.

62
00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:18.194
"Now we know that we can't expect anything from that man," said Ned after he'd heard my story.

63
00:06:18.195 --> 00:06:23.273
"The Nautilus is nearing Long Island, and we'll escape no matter what the weather may be."

64
00:06:23.274 --> 00:06:28.519
But the weather became more and more threatening until a hurricane hit us a few days later.

65
00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:33.860
I thought the captain would take the Nautilus underwater, but he insisted on remaining on the surface.

66
00:06:33.861 --> 00:06:40.408
I went up to the platform and found that he had tied himself to the railing to keep from being swept overboard by the raging sea.

67
00:06:40.409 --> 00:06:43.011
The sky was streaked with lightning.

68
00:06:43.012 --> 00:06:49.924
I could not bear the brightness of it, but the captain looked right at it and seemed to envy the spirit of the storm.

69
00:06:49.925 --> 00:06:57.537
The submarine tilted dreadfully, raising its bow into the air as thunder and lightning struck around us.
